{"path":"/work/workspace/deliverable/apa_redline.md","oldString":"> 9.3 Escrow. $310,000 (10% of the Purchase Price) shall be deposited in escrow for eighteen (18) months (the "General Escrow"), securing Seller's indemnification obligations under Section 7.1, and shall be the sole source of recovery therefor absent Fundamental Representations or fraud. Of that amount, $150,000 is designated the "Employment Escrow Amount" securing the Worker Classification Special Indemnity under Section 7.5, which shall be released on the [18]-month anniversary to the extent no claim has been noticed. No claim noticed in good faith before the applicable release date may be paid from escrow except as finally determined. Escrow fees are borne [equally / by Buyer].

**Why:** keeps buyer's 10% comfort where it belongs (the negotiated caps), makes escrow the primary recovery source so judgments can't run past it for general claims, and puts the agreed $150,000 special-indemnity cap behind real money so buyer can accept the cap instead of demanding full-price coverage. The single change that makes this all work is the 7.4(b) cap fix; escrow structure follows it.","newString":"> 9.3 Escrow. $310,000 (10% of the Purchase Price) shall be deposited in escrow for eighteen (18) months (the "General Escrow"), securing Seller's indemnification obligations under Section 7.1, and shall be the sole source of recovery therefor absent Fundamental Representations or fraud. Of that amount, $150,000 [or $200,000, if the Special Indemnity cap moves per the fallback in §7.4(b)] is designated the "Employment Escrow Amount" securing the Worker Classification Special Indemnity under Section 7.4(b), which shall be released on the [18]-month anniversary to the extent no claim has been noticed. No claim noticed in good faith before the applicable release date may be paid from escrow except as finally determined. Escrow fees are borne [equally / by Buyer].

**Why:** keeps buyer's 10% comfort where it belongs (the negotiated caps), makes escrow the primary recovery source so judgments can't run past it for general claims, and puts the agreed special-indemnity cap behind real money so buyer can accept the cap instead of demanding full-price coverage. The designated tranche must track the final 7.4(b) cap — if the cap moves to $200,000, the designated tranche moves with it (that still leaves $110,000 of escrow for everything else, against a $310,000 general cap). The single change that makes this all work is the 7.4(b) cap fix; escrow structure follows it."}

{"newString":"# APA redline — Brightline Cabinetry / Lone Star Millwork Holdings LLC

Buyer's draft, key sections. Position is sell-side. **Internal working document:** the proposed clause language (block quotes) is what we would circulate; the "Why" notes and negotiation instructions (e.g., the $150,000-hold/$200,000-fallback range in §7.4(b)) are attorney work product and must be stripped before anything goes to buyer's counsel. **Assumption stated up front:** the excerpt does not state the Purchase Price; the escrow clause ($310,000 = "10%") implies **Purchase Price = $310,000 / 0.10 = $3,100,000**, so the 7.4(b) general cap = $310,000 and the §4.12 carve-out cap = $3,100,000. All percentages below use that figure; confirm (QUESTIONS.md Q1).","oldString":"# APA redline — Brightline Cabinetry / Lone Star Millwork Holdings LLC

